1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:01,690
All over the world,

2
00:00:01,730 --> 00:00:03,370
people are quietly using

3
00:00:03,410 --> 00:00:05,480
AI to make life better,

4
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,290
for people, for animals,

5
00:00:07,330 --> 00:00:09,360
for the planet itself.

6
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:10,790
Most of these projects,

7
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:12,920
you've probably never heard of.

8
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:14,510
In India, a train

9
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:15,510
brakes in the dark

10
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:16,450
for elephants its

11
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,940
driver can't see.

12
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:19,650
In California, a man

13
00:00:19,690 --> 00:00:20,790
silenced by illness

14
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:23,700
speaks again, in his own voice.

15
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:25,680
It makes people cry

16
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:27,750
who have not heard me in a while.

17
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:29,860
And in Peru's desert,

18
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:31,240
ancient drawings are

19
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:32,450
coming to light.

20
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,090
All of it is real.

21
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:34,920
It's the same kind

22
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:35,800
of tool that writes

23
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:37,970
your emails and picks your ads.

24
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:39,180
A few people pointed

25
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:41,320
it at something far bigger.

26
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,380
This is what AI

27
00:00:42,420 --> 00:00:43,870
should be used for.

28
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:44,600
What do all these

29
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:46,250
tools have in common?

30
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:47,100
A first clue:

31
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,500
the last of them learned

32
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:49,640
where floods strike

33
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:51,450
from old news reports.

34
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:54,470
At the far edge of New Zealand,

35
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:56,030
a tiny radio station

36
00:00:56,070 --> 00:00:57,110
set out to build

37
00:00:57,150 --> 00:00:59,540
something no tech giant had:

38
00:00:59,790 --> 00:01:00,730
a machine that

39
00:01:00,770 --> 00:01:02,920
understands spoken Māori.

40
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:04,310
To learn a language,

41
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:06,360
a machine has to hear it,

42
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:08,380
for hours and hours.

43
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,050
The station asked its own people.

44
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,260
In just ten days, they read enough

45
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,200
Māori into their

46
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,810
phones to build it.

47
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:17,740
A global data

48
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,080
company wanted recordings

49
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:20,470
like these too,

50
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:21,970
and offered to pay.

51
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:23,970
The station said no.

52
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,130
Peter Lucas Jones runs it.

53
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,310
This is how he explained the no.

54
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,450
We're a charity,

55
00:01:30,490 --> 00:01:32,290
we're a Māori charity.

56
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:33,920
And so we look at ways

57
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:35,520
to maintain sovereignty

58
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,620
because we know what it means

59
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:39,150
to lose it.

60
00:01:39,190 --> 00:01:43,260
We know that data is the new land.

61
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:45,330
A machine can't keep

62
00:01:45,370 --> 00:01:46,740
a language alive.

63
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:48,420
Only speakers can.

64
00:01:48,710 --> 00:01:49,470
But this time,

65
00:01:49,510 --> 00:01:51,740
the speakers own the machine.

66
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:55,210
That machine was built to listen.

67
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:57,160
In Australia, another AI

68
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:58,880
was built to waste time.

69
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,130
On purpose.

70
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:02,300
Not yours.

71
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:03,950
A phone scammer's.

72
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:05,260
A researcher there once

73
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:06,620
kept a scammer on the line

74
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:07,1000
for about forty minutes,

75
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:10,110
during a family picnic.

76
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:11,020
All that time,

77
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:12,160
the scammer wasn't

78
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:13,810
robbing anyone else.

79
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:15,700
Now his company does it all day,

80
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,700
with AI victims:

81
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:19,120
chatty strangers with all

82
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:20,820
the time in the world.

83
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:21,900
One of Australia's big

84
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:23,260
phone networks already

85
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:24,420
sends scam calls

86
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:26,230
straight to these bots.

87
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:30,090
Here, the AI wins by being slow.

88
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,970
Over Wyoming, it has to be fast.

89
00:02:34,310 --> 00:02:36,070
Wind turbines make clean power,

90
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:38,560
and they kill eagles.

91
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:39,490
Stop them all,

92
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:41,480
and you lose the power.

93
00:02:41,670 --> 00:02:43,960
Stop none, and eagles die.

94
00:02:44,330 --> 00:02:45,340
What would you do?

95
00:02:46,790 --> 00:02:48,490
On one wind farm in Wyoming,

96
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:51,340
cameras on towers watch the sky.

97
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:52,610
In the blink of an eye,

98
00:02:52,650 --> 00:02:55,920
an AI decides: eagle or not.

99
00:02:56,270 --> 00:02:57,130
Only the turbines

100
00:02:57,170 --> 00:02:59,390
in its path slow down.

101
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:00,370
A study credited

102
00:03:00,410 --> 00:03:01,570
the cameras with cutting

103
00:03:01,610 --> 00:03:02,630
eagle deaths there

104
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:05,050
by more than four in five.

105
00:03:05,090 --> 00:03:06,850
Other scientists say one wind

106
00:03:06,890 --> 00:03:08,680
farm can't prove that yet.

107
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:12,120
Here, one miss can cost an eagle.

108
00:03:12,370 --> 00:03:13,510
In Peru, a machine

109
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:15,840
was wrong almost every time,

110
00:03:15,890 --> 00:03:17,260
and it still worked.

111
00:03:18,570 --> 00:03:19,940
The Nazca desert is famous

112
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:21,910
for its giant drawings.

113
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:23,500
An AI combed aerial

114
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:25,180
photos of it and flagged

115
00:03:25,220 --> 00:03:27,470
tens of thousands of spots.

116
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:29,890
Almost all of them were nothing.

117
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:31,580
Archaeologists checked only

118
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:32,940
the best leads on site,

119
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:34,060
and nearly doubled

120
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:35,570
the figures known there.

121
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:36,820
The new ones are small,

122
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:37,840
and they sit close

123
00:03:37,880 --> 00:03:39,550
to ancient footpaths.

124
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:40,970
The team's reading:

125
00:03:41,240 --> 00:03:42,100
the giant drawings

126
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:44,040
were made for community rituals.

127
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:45,000
The small ones,

128
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:46,970
for people walking past.

129
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:50,650
The AI didn't need to be right.

130
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:51,740
It only had to tell

131
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,470
people where to look.

132
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:55,140
But what happens when

133
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,020
the machine points,

134
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:58,170
and no one looks?

135
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:01,590
Methane is invisible, and it heats

136
00:04:01,630 --> 00:04:03,050
the planet far faster

137
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:04,720
than carbon dioxide.

138
00:04:04,970 --> 00:04:06,250
To catch every leak,

139
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:07,430
you'd need eyes over

140
00:04:07,470 --> 00:04:08,920
the whole planet.

141
00:04:09,760 --> 00:04:11,210
They're already up there.

142
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:12,700
Satellites built to watch

143
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:14,100
fields and forests

144
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,410
can catch methane's faint shadow.

145
00:04:16,780 --> 00:04:18,400
Every day, an AI searches

146
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:20,090
their pictures for it.

147
00:04:20,280 --> 00:04:21,980
Experts check, and the UN

148
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:23,820
warns the country.

149
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:25,700
In Algeria, one leak had been

150
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:26,920
visible from space

151
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:28,610
for over a decade.

152
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,120
Months after the alerts,

153
00:04:30,160 --> 00:04:31,270
it stopped.

154
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,000
But that's the exception.

155
00:04:34,070 --> 00:04:35,410
Nearly nine in ten alerts

156
00:04:35,450 --> 00:04:37,220
still get no answer.

157
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:38,130
Two years ago,

158
00:04:38,170 --> 00:04:40,220
almost none got one.

159
00:04:40,690 --> 00:04:42,940
Seeing isn't saving.

160
00:04:44,250 --> 00:04:45,800
Casey Harrell knows exactly

161
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,390
what he wants to say.

162
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:49,640
His brain still sends the words.

163
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,290
But his lips no longer follow.

164
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:55,820
He has ALS He said it felt

165
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:57,590
like being trapped.

166
00:04:57,720 --> 00:04:58,880
How do you hear a sentence

167
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:01,790
that never leaves the body?

168
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:04,340
Surgeons placed tiny sensors where

169
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,440
his brain steers

170
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:07,230
his lips and tongue.

171
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:08,560
An AI learned which

172
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:10,870
signals mean which sounds.

173
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,320
It doesn't read his thoughts.

174
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,890
Only the words he's trying to say.

175
00:05:16,530 --> 00:05:17,780
Then it speaks them,

176
00:05:17,820 --> 00:05:19,120
in his own voice,

177
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:20,660
rebuilt from recordings

178
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:22,730
made before he fell ill.

179
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:24,410
This is that voice.

180
00:05:25,110 --> 00:05:26,230
People who do not have the ability

181
00:05:26,270 --> 00:05:27,620
to communicate well or quickly

182
00:05:27,660 --> 00:05:30,190
are often isolated and lonely.

183
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:31,340
Having something like this will

184
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:32,570
give them hope and opportunity

185
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:33,960
to be more of themselves.

186
00:05:34,630 --> 00:05:36,790
It's one man, in a research study,

187
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:37,910
not yet a treatment

188
00:05:37,950 --> 00:05:39,440
anyone can get.

189
00:05:39,730 --> 00:05:41,890
But at home, in under two years,

190
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:43,250
he spoke almost two

191
00:05:43,290 --> 00:05:45,180
million words with it.

192
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:46,940
This channel exists

193
00:05:46,980 --> 00:05:48,450
for one reason:

194
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:49,440
so that the arrival

195
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:50,540
of superintelligence

196
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:52,590
goes well for everybody.

197
00:05:52,740 --> 00:05:53,400
Want that too?

198
00:05:53,440 --> 00:05:54,630
Then subscribe.

199
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:57,150
Casey wants to be heard.

200
00:05:57,500 --> 00:05:58,220
Out at sea,

201
00:05:58,260 --> 00:05:59,320
some ships would

202
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:01,090
rather not be seen.

203
00:06:02,370 --> 00:06:03,270
Big ships are supposed

204
00:06:03,310 --> 00:06:04,890
to broadcast where they are,

205
00:06:04,930 --> 00:06:06,350
on a radio built so

206
00:06:06,390 --> 00:06:07,520
they don't collide.

207
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:09,890
Take a guess: how many industrial

208
00:06:09,930 --> 00:06:11,070
fishing boats stay

209
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:12,800
off the public map?

210
00:06:13,490 --> 00:06:14,830
An AI compared years

211
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:15,890
of radar pictures

212
00:06:15,930 --> 00:06:18,320
from space with those broadcasts.

213
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:21,420
The answer: about three in four.

214
00:06:21,790 --> 00:06:23,530
Dark doesn't mean illegal.

215
00:06:23,570 --> 00:06:24,870
But it's where the worst

216
00:06:24,910 --> 00:06:26,380
things can hide.

217
00:06:26,610 --> 00:06:28,630
Another AI reads how ships move,

218
00:06:28,670 --> 00:06:30,530
and flags those whose crews

219
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:32,660
may not be free to go home.

220
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:35,180
That's a risk, not a verdict.

221
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:36,790
A ship can hide

222
00:06:36,830 --> 00:06:39,020
by switching its radio off.

223
00:06:39,310 --> 00:06:41,940
A tsunami hides in plain sight.

224
00:06:43,290 --> 00:06:44,380
In the open ocean,

225
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:46,250
it's barely a ripple.

226
00:06:46,460 --> 00:06:47,360
How do you see it coming

227
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:49,110
before it reaches land?

228
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:50,880
Look up.

229
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:52,300
The rising water shoves

230
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:53,710
the air above it.

231
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:55,280
That push climbs all

232
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:56,420
the way to space,

233
00:06:56,460 --> 00:06:58,040
where it makes GPS signals

234
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,250
arrive a tiny bit late.

235
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:01,660
The same signals your phone

236
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,310
uses to find your way.

237
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:04,680
Another clue.

238
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:06,340
An AI learned to read

239
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:08,390
the tsunami's fingerprint in them.

240
00:07:08,650 --> 00:07:10,830
NASA switched it on the day before

241
00:07:10,870 --> 00:07:11,950
a giant earthquake

242
00:07:11,990 --> 00:07:13,460
struck off Russia.

243
00:07:14,110 --> 00:07:15,750
NASA's replay shows it catching

244
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:17,850
the wave in the sky over Hawaii,

245
00:07:17,890 --> 00:07:19,170
half an hour before

246
00:07:19,210 --> 00:07:21,140
the first tide gauge there.

247
00:07:21,270 --> 00:07:23,060
It's still a research tool.

248
00:07:23,170 --> 00:07:23,930
Half an hour can

249
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:25,780
warn a whole coast.

250
00:07:25,870 --> 00:07:27,820
But you can't warn an elephant.

251
00:07:29,150 --> 00:07:30,800
You're driving a train at night,

252
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:31,760
through an elephant

253
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:33,890
corridor in southern India.

254
00:07:34,280 --> 00:07:34,880
By the time your

255
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:36,060
lights find an elephant

256
00:07:36,100 --> 00:07:36,880
on the track,

257
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:38,810
it's too late to stop.

258
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:41,970
Unless the track sees it first.

259
00:07:42,280 --> 00:07:43,440
Cameras on towers along

260
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:45,110
the line see heat.

261
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:47,500
An AI spots the elephant and warns

262
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:49,150
the driver and the rangers.

263
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:50,380
This time, the warning

264
00:07:50,420 --> 00:07:51,590
gets an answer:

265
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,380
the train brakes.

266
00:07:53,420 --> 00:07:53,840
On the stretch

267
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:55,460
it watches, officials say,

268
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:56,940
no train has killed

269
00:07:56,980 --> 00:07:58,990
an elephant since it went live.

270
00:07:59,630 --> 00:08:01,710
Elephants are the giants here.

271
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:02,980
But across India,

272
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:04,800
a far smaller animal

273
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:05,680
kills about a hundred

274
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:07,820
times more people.

275
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:09,430
It hides in the grass.

276
00:08:10,770 --> 00:08:11,450
The snake.

277
00:08:11,490 --> 00:08:12,970
Its bite is still

278
00:08:13,010 --> 00:08:14,010
treated with medicine

279
00:08:14,050 --> 00:08:15,090
made from the blood

280
00:08:15,130 --> 00:08:16,860
of horses and sheep.

281
00:08:17,230 --> 00:08:18,750
What if you could skip the horse,

282
00:08:18,790 --> 00:08:20,470
and design a molecule no

283
00:08:20,510 --> 00:08:22,500
living thing has ever made?

284
00:08:23,050 --> 00:08:26,020
A lab asked an AI to do just that.

285
00:08:26,270 --> 00:08:28,190
It designed brand new proteins,

286
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:29,690
shaped to grab a snake's

287
00:08:29,730 --> 00:08:31,560
toxin and switch it off.

288
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:33,730
In mice, they worked.

289
00:08:33,770 --> 00:08:34,790
Most survived a dose

290
00:08:34,830 --> 00:08:36,610
that should have killed them.

291
00:08:36,650 --> 00:08:38,250
So far only in mice,

292
00:08:38,290 --> 00:08:40,430
and only against single toxins,

293
00:08:40,470 --> 00:08:41,960
not whole venom.

294
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:43,950
A designed protein can

295
00:08:43,990 --> 00:08:45,940
stop a poison in the blood.

296
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:48,780
But can a machine change a mind?

297
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:51,700
Everyone knows you can't argue

298
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:52,560
someone out of

299
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:54,150
a conspiracy theory.

300
00:08:54,520 --> 00:08:56,170
Researchers tried anyway:

301
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:56,960
they let believers

302
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,870
argue with an AI

303
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,600
Just an ordinary chatbot,

304
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,430
the kind that writes emails.

305
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:04,120
It answered each person's

306
00:09:04,160 --> 00:09:06,210
own reasons with evidence.

307
00:09:06,460 --> 00:09:07,580
David Rand, one

308
00:09:07,620 --> 00:09:08,500
of the researchers,

309
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:10,110
on why it worked:

310
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:11,520
And when you look at what

311
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:13,330
the model does, it just sort of

312
00:09:13,370 --> 00:09:15,980
politely presented

313
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:17,750
non-conspiratorial explanations

314
00:09:17,790 --> 00:09:19,340
and contradictory facts

315
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:20,380
and evidence.

316
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:22,730
After one short conversation,

317
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:25,220
belief dropped by about a fifth.

318
00:09:25,490 --> 00:09:27,660
Months later, it was still down.

319
00:09:27,890 --> 00:09:28,970
The authors later found

320
00:09:29,010 --> 00:09:31,040
errors in their own data.

321
00:09:31,250 --> 00:09:33,900
Corrected, the effect still held.

322
00:09:34,530 --> 00:09:35,950
In a newer study,

323
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:37,690
not yet peer reviewed,

324
00:09:37,730 --> 00:09:39,630
the same team flipped it:

325
00:09:39,670 --> 00:09:40,650
a chatbot argued

326
00:09:40,690 --> 00:09:42,570
for the conspiracy.

327
00:09:42,610 --> 00:09:44,920
Belief went up just as strongly.

328
00:09:45,490 --> 00:09:46,430
Same tool.

329
00:09:46,470 --> 00:09:47,940
Opposite aim.

330
00:09:48,490 --> 00:09:49,450
What do all these

331
00:09:49,490 --> 00:09:51,020
tools have in common?

332
00:09:52,320 --> 00:09:53,080
Flash floods are

333
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:54,770
the deadliest floods.

334
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,220
They often hit places where

335
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:58,090
no one measures the water,

336
00:09:58,260 --> 00:09:59,480
so a machine has almost

337
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:01,130
nothing to learn from.

338
00:10:02,210 --> 00:10:03,500
Except the news.

339
00:10:03,830 --> 00:10:05,450
A team at Google pulled millions

340
00:10:05,490 --> 00:10:07,250
of past floods out of old

341
00:10:07,290 --> 00:10:09,500
news reports, to teach an AI

342
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:11,560
where flash floods strike.

343
00:10:11,790 --> 00:10:12,550
For big rivers,

344
00:10:12,590 --> 00:10:14,200
Google's flood forecasts

345
00:10:14,290 --> 00:10:16,640
already reach up to a week ahead.

346
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:17,770
The warnings show up

347
00:10:17,810 --> 00:10:19,580
in Search, in Maps,

348
00:10:19,650 --> 00:10:22,060
and as alerts on ordinary phones.

349
00:10:22,670 --> 00:10:23,980
Maybe on yours.

350
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:27,040
But a forecast isn't a rescue.

351
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:28,810
Like those methane alerts,

352
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:29,610
it only works

353
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:31,640
if someone acts in time.

354
00:10:32,990 --> 00:10:34,180
Satellites built to watch

355
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:36,010
fields and forests.

356
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:37,400
A radio built so

357
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:39,110
ships don't collide.

358
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:42,110
GPS, built to find your way.

359
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:44,010
An everyday chatbot.

360
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,570
Old news, written for readers.

361
00:10:47,140 --> 00:10:49,130
None of it was built for this.

362
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:50,020
That's what these

363
00:10:50,060 --> 00:10:51,590
tools have in common.

364
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:52,440
The difference between

365
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:53,520
writing your emails

366
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:54,780
and saving a life

367
00:10:54,820 --> 00:10:56,470
was never the tool.

368
00:10:56,540 --> 00:10:57,670
It was the aim.

369
00:10:57,820 --> 00:10:59,710
The tool is already here.

370
00:10:59,780 --> 00:11:00,780
The question is what

371
00:11:00,820 --> 00:11:02,130
we point it at.

372
00:11:02,830 --> 00:11:04,480
That's why this channel exists:

373
00:11:04,520 --> 00:11:05,960
so that superintelligence

374
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,850
goes well for everyone.

375
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:08,660
Want that too?

376
00:11:08,700 --> 00:11:09,910
Then subscribe.

